<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        When a session was last used, so that leaving one alone can end it.

        WHY THE TIME OF THE LAST REQUEST IS STORED
        A signed token says who is holding it and when it was issued, and nothing else, so the token cannot answer
        "has this session been idle for half an hour?". Holding the answer in memory would answer it only for
        whichever node served the last request, would lose it on every deploy, and would mean the timeout never
        fires for a session that moved between nodes - which is the case the timeout exists for.

        WHY IT IS ONE TIMESTAMP PER ACCOUNT AND NOT ONE PER SESSION
        Per-session would need the token registry this design deliberately avoids. The requirement is a session
        timeout, and two sessions for one account belong to one person: signing out one of them signing out both
        is a consequence worth stating rather than hiding.

        WHY NOTHING IS BACKFILLED
        Nullable, and null means "no request has been recorded yet". The rule then ages the session from the
        token's own issue time, so every token that exists when this lands is judged from when it was issued
        rather than being thrown out by a column added underneath it.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260930130000-1" author="hms">
        <comment>Add jhi_user.last_activity_at for the idle session timeout</comment>
        <addColumn tableName="jhi_user">
            <column
                name="last_activity_at"
                type="timestamp"
                remarks="When a request last arrived on this account's session; a session unused for longer than the idle timeout is refused">
                <constraints nullable="true"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
